Lab 2: Managing Keystone (Identity Service)
Objectives
Configure and manage the Keystone identity service in OpenStack
Create and manage users, roles, and projects in Keystone
Integrate Keystone with external identity providers
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment (DevStack or production deployment)
Administrative access to the OpenStack controller node
Basic Linux command line proficiency
Python 3.x installed
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python3-openstackclient)
Setup Requirements
SSH access to your OpenStack controller node
Admin credentials for your OpenStack deployment
Internet access for package installation
Task 1: Setting Up Keystone and Configuring Authentication
1.1 Verify Keystone Service Status
sudo systemctl status openstack-keystone
Expected Output: Service should be active (running)

Troubleshooting Tip: If service isn't running:

sudo systemctl start openstack-keystone
sudo systemctl enable openstack-keystone
1.2 Configure Keystone Authentication
Edit the Keystone configuration file:

sudo vi /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Add/Modify these sections:

[identity]
driver = sql

[token]
provider = fernet
Restart the service:

sudo systemctl restart openstack-keystone
1.3 Initialize the Keystone Database
sudo su -s /bin/sh -c "keystone-manage db_sync" keystone
Verification:

mysql -u keystone -p -e "USE keystone; SHOW TABLES;"
Task 2: Creating and Managing Users, Roles, and Projects
2.1 Create a New Project
openstack project create --description "Development Project" dev_project
Verify:

openstack project list
2.2 Create a New Role
openstack role create developer
Verify:

openstack role list
2.3 Create a New User
openstack user create --password-prompt dev_user
Expected Outcome: You'll be prompted to set and confirm a password

2.4 Assign Role to User in Project
openstack role add --project dev_project --user dev_user developer
Verify the assignment:

openstack role assignment list --user dev_user --project dev_project
Task 3: Integrating Keystone with LDAP
3.1 Install Required Packages
sudo apt-get install -y python3-ldap ldap-utils
3.2 Configure LDAP Integration
Edit Keystone configuration:

sudo vi /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Add LDAP configuration:

[identity]
driver = ldap

[ldap]
url = ldap://your.ldap.server
user_tree_dn = ou=Users,dc=example,dc=com
user_objectclass = inetOrgPerson
3.3 Restart Keystone
sudo systemctl restart openstack-keystone
3.4 Verify LDAP Integration
openstack user list
Expected Outcome: Should show users from your LDAP directory

Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Successfully configured the Keystone identity service
Created and managed OpenStack projects, users, and roles
Integrated Keystone with an external LDAP identity provider
These skills are fundamental for managing authentication and authorization in OpenStack environments. The configurations you've implemented provide the foundation for secure access control across all OpenStack services.

